9. Pricing and currency policy — unresolved decisions
The existing UI had a conflict between fixed dropdown prices (₦35,000 and ₦500,000) and “flexible service fee” wording. Do not hard-code these as final without product-owner confirmation.
Before implementation, decide:
Are service prices fixed, or does each request require an Admin quote?
Is bank transfer strictly NGN?
Is there a Pi-denominated amount for each service, or is Pi amount quoted separately?
If Pi amount is derived from NGN price, what approved exchange-rate source, timestamp and quote expiry rule will be used? Do not assume a market rate or hard-code a conversion without policy.
What are cancellation, refund, duplicate payment, short payment, overpayment and dispute rules?
Who can verify bank statements, and what constitutes the approved evidence source?
Keep an immutable price/config snapshot on each intent so future pricing or account changes do not rewrite an existing payment record.
10. Security, privacy and audit
Reuse existing Admin Auth, role enforcement and MFA/AAL2 requirement; verify enforcement on the server/RPC, not only UI.
Supabase secret/service-role credentials stay only in trusted server/Edge Function environments; never ship them to HTML/JS or write them to logs.
Enable RLS where relevant, and explicitly design grants and allow/deny policies. RLS with no policy is not a complete test plan; test each verb for anon, authenticated, Admin with/without correct role, and cross-network cases.
User reads are scoped to their own requests/intents by server-verified identity and/or secure DB policy.
Admin notes, verification source details and evidence are private. Use least-privilege reads and short-lived signed URLs.
Rate-limit evidence submissions and reconciliation-sensitive endpoints; validate file content/size server-side, generate storage object paths server-side.
Use CSRF/origin handling appropriate to existing API architecture; avoid logging full receipts, full bank details or provider secrets.
Use idempotency, row locking/atomic transitions, unique provider references and audit events to defend concurrent/repeated calls.
Environment comes from trusted backend config. No request-body network switch can redirect Mainnet actions to Testnet or vice versa.
Supabase security references:
RLS guidance: https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/postgres/row-level-security
API key safety: https://supabase.com/docs/guides/getting-started/api-keys
Secure data guidance: https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/secure-data
11. Acceptance tests before staging approval
Admin bank settings
Draft/unverified account is never returned as a payable bank destination.
Config cannot activate until verified and approved.
The maker cannot self-activate their own bank destination if maker-checker is enforced.
Disabled/retired config is no longer offered for new intents.
A newly active bank version does not alter an existing intent's snapshot.
Admin without the required role or AAL2 cannot create, verify, activate, disable or reconcile settings.
Bank transfer
A fabricated receipt/reference never confirms a payment by itself.
Amount/currency/destination mismatch is rejected or left pending.
One valid transfer cannot be confirmed against multiple requests.
Repeated submission/reconciliation has no duplicate effect.
Receipt is inaccessible to another user or via a public URL.
If no valid active config exists, bank transfer is unavailable and no bank intent can be created.
Pi payment
Client success is insufficient to mark paid.
Pi payment ID from another user's intent or an unrelated Core Project payment is rejected.
Invalid amount/metadata/network/status fails closed.
Approve/complete retries are idempotent; incomplete payments can be reconciled.
Server API Key and secret credentials are absent from browser assets, client responses and logs.
Separation and regression
Mainnet cannot read/write Testnet dApp-service data, nor vice versa.
Request approval does not automatically confirm payment; confirmed payment does not automatically approve or complete a service.
Existing Core Project investment/liquidity flow continues to require its own add_liquidity checks.
No changes to Dock Navigation, page-auth-guard.js, environment-core.js, or shared auth/Supabase cores unless a separate reviewed patch proves necessity.
Regression tests cover current services listing, request submission, My dApp Requests, Admin login/MFA and other existing app pages.
12. Controlled implementation sequence
Review design: approve or revise price policy, Admin roles/maker-checker, verification sources, refund rules.
Read-only re-audit: confirm current repo SHAs, active Admin auth helpers, API conventions and live DB schema before patching.
Branch-only coding: add service-specific API module/UI and migration file; avoid unrelated refactors and shared core changes.
Local/static tests: lint/syntax, API contract tests, state-transition tests, RLS tests, idempotency and authorization-negative tests.
Testnet/staging QA: use only test data and supported Pi test flow; no live bank account or real funds.
Patch review: provide complete changed-file list/diff, migrations, tests, rollback plan, and unresolved decisions for user approval.
Mainnet deployment gate: no production migration/deployment until explicit approval, verified company bank account, finalized pricing/terms, Pi Developer Portal readiness, and successful staging sign-off.
Bank go-live: first activate bank destination only after independent verification; run small controlled reconciliation test and confirm statement matching before public enablement.
13. Matakin gaba bayan wannan takarda
Wannan shi ne contract/design deliverable, ba implementation ba. Bayan an duba shi, mataki mafi aminci shi ne:
kulle business rules da har yanzu ba a yanke shawara a kansu ba (farashi, NGN/Pi pricing, refund, bank verification authority); sannan
a samar da branch-only implementation patch tare da migration da tests, a duba diff kafin a taɓa Mainnet.
Babu canjin production da aka yi a wannan mataki.
